// It accepts a single SEXP argument — typically a length-1 real or
// integer vector — and returns the C double value of its first element.
// Unlike REAL(x), which returns a pointer to the entire element buffer,
// asReal extracts and returns only the first element as a scalar double,
// performing a coercion if necessary (e.g., from INTSXP to double).
//
// In rpart's source files, asReal is used exclusively to extract scalar
// floating-point configuration parameters from .Call input arguments,
// converting single-element SEXP wrappers into plain double values for
// use as function arguments or local variable initializers.
//
// asReal is not an R Interpreter Item; it requires no running R
// interpreter and no function-pointer bridge.
//
// Observed usage across all CSV rows:
//
//   P1 — Pass scalar double as a direct function argument:
//          rpartexp2.c:48  Rpartexp2(n, REAL(dtimes), asReal(eps), INTEGER(keep));
//          asReal(eps) converts a length-1 REALSXP to double and passes it as
//          the third positional argument to Rpartexp2 (declared double eps).
//          No intermediate variable is used; the compiler loads the double
//          directly from eps->data[0] into the argument slot.
//
//   P2 — Assign scalar double to a local variable:
//          xpred.c:76  toprisk = asReal(toprisk2);
//          toprisk is declared 'double toprisk;' earlier in xpred().
//          toprisk2 is a .Call input parameter (length-1 REALSXP).
//          The double result is stored in toprisk for later use in the
//          function body.
//
// Both patterns share the identical fake implementation.  The split
// reflects the calling context (direct argument vs. local-variable
// assignment), not any difference in the function itself.
